    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aurangzeb.jpg" alt="Aurangzeb" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aurangzeb-portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Darbarscene.jpg: Cordanrad derivative work: Rani nurmai (talk), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> State grew out of the Mughal administration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, the plateau of south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> in the 1680s, when the sultanates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> fell. Its founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-jah-i">Mir Qamar-ud-din Khan</a> (1671–1748), was a noble of Central Asian descent who had served Aurangzeb and received the title Nizam-ul-Mulk. Appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> of the six Mughal provinces of the Deccan in 1713, he seized control of the region again in 1720 and then served as imperial chief minister (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wazir">wazir</a></em>) from 1722, but he became disillusioned with the factional politics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. In 1724 he left the capital without permission, and when the governor favoured by the court, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mubariz-khan">Mubariz Khan</a>, opposed him, Nizam-ul-Mulk defeated and killed him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shakar-kheda">Battle of Shakar Kheda</a> in October 1724. From then on he governed the Deccan as a hereditary ruler, while continuing to acknowledge the Mughal emperor, issuing coins in the emperor’s name and avoiding the title of king. He received the title Asaf Jah, which gave the dynasty its name, and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangabad">Aurangabad</a> his capital. The state that emerged was large but under constant pressure from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, and its boundaries shifted repeatedly during the eighteenth century. In 1763 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-ali-khan">Nizam Ali Khan</a> moved the capital to the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-shahi-dynasty">Qutb Shahi</a> city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh">Hyderabad</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh.jpg" alt="Hyderabad, Sindh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh">Hyderabad, Sindh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mir_Karam_Ali_Khan_Talpur_Tombs_were_restored_in_2016_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Waheed.chandio, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hyderabad was the largest and most populous of the Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, covering some 214,000 square kilometres and home to more than sixteen million people at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1941. Its society was marked by a sharp divide between rulers and ruled. The court, nobility and much of the administration were Muslim and Urdu-speaking, while over four-fifths of the population was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and spoke Telugu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a>. Great nobles, including the <em>paigah</em> families and other <em>jagirdars</em>, held vast estates, and land in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a> districts was dominated by landlords (<em>deshmukhs</em>) who extracted rent and forced labour (<em>vetti</em>) from peasants. The economy was overwhelmingly agricultural, producing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millets</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and oilseeds, and the region had been famous for diamonds from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> mines, though these were largely worked out by the eighteenth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh">Hyderabad</a> city was a centre of craft production and banking, where Hindu merchant and banking families financed the state. In the late nineteenth and twentieth centuries the state built its own railway, postal service and currency, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osman-sagar">Osman Sagar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himayat-sagar">Himayat Sagar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> after the Musi flood of 1908, and textile and other industries, while the personal wealth of the nizams in jewels and gold became legendary.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kharda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kharda.jpg" alt="Battle of Kharda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kharda">Battle of Kharda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kharda_Fort.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rushikesh Awatade, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-jahi-dynasty">Asaf Jahi</a> state relied on a cavalry army of Mughal type, led by nobles who maintained troops from their land assignments and supplemented by artillery and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, including Arab and Afghan soldiers. It proved weaker than the mobile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> armies. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-palkhed">Battle of Palkhed</a> in 1728 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baji-rao-i">Peshwa Baji Rao I</a> outmanoeuvred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-jah-i">Nizam-ul-Mulk</a> and forced him to recognise Maratha rights to collect revenue (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauth">chauth</a></em> and <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardeshmukhi">sardeshmukhi</a></em>) in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, and at the turn of 1737–1738 Baji Rao defeated him again near Bhopal. After Asaf Jah’s death in 1748 his sons and grandson fought over the succession with French and British backing, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salabat-jung">Salabat Jung</a> owed his throne to the French commander the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-de-bussy">Marquis de Bussy</a>. Defeats by the Marathas at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-udgir">Udgir</a> in 1760 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kharda">Kharda</a> in 1795 cost the state large territories. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh">Hyderabad</a> fought alongside the British against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> of Mysore in 1790–1792 and again in 1799. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-ali-khan">Nizam Ali Khan</a> built up a French-trained corps under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-raymond">Michel Raymond</a>, but in 1798 the British required its disbandment under the subsidiary alliance. Thereafter British troops stationed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secunderabad">Secunderabad</a> cantonment and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-contingent">Hyderabad Contingent</a>, paid for by the state, guaranteed its security. In 1948 the state army, assisted by the irregular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/razakars-hyderabad">Razakars</a>, collapsed within days before the Indian army.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/salar-jung-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salar-jung-i.jpg" alt="Salar Jung I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/salar-jung-i">Salar Jung I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Salar_Jung.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">undated photo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The nizams ruled as autocrats through a chief minister (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwan</a></em>), a system inherited from Mughal provincial government, with Persian as the language of administration until it was replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> in the 1880s. The rulers formally acknowledged the Mughal emperor until 1858, and afterwards they were the foremost of the princes under British paramountcy, entitled to a 21-gun salute and, from 1918, the style ‘His Exalted Highness’. The subsidiary alliance of 1798, extended in 1800, placed a British Resident at the court and made the state’s foreign relations a British concern; to pay for the troops <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh">Hyderabad</a> ceded the territories later known as the Ceded Districts in 1800, and in 1853 it assigned the revenues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a> to the British. The most significant reformer was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salar-jung-i">Salar Jung I</a>, chief minister from 1853 to 1883, who reorganised revenue collection, created district administration on modern lines and founded new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> for justice, police and education. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahbub-ali-khan">Mir Mahbub Ali Khan</a> (r. 1869–1911) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-osman-ali-khan">Mir Osman Ali Khan</a> (r. 1911–1948) presided over a modernising bureaucracy, though political participation remained limited: an Executive Council was created in 1919, but legislative bodies had only advisory or restricted powers. Government favoured Urdu-speaking Muslims for office, a source of growing tension in the twentieth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/salar-jung-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salar-jung-iii.jpg" alt="Salar Jung III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/salar-jung-iii">Salar Jung III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salarjungiii.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rgajavelli, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh">Hyderabad</a> was the most important successor state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> in the south and preserved a version of Mughal court culture until the mid-twentieth century. The city of Hyderabad became a major centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> literature and of a composite Deccani culture blending Persianate, Telugu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> elements, visible in its cuisine, dress and architecture. The nizams and their nobles were patrons of poets, scholars and builders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osmania-university">Osmania University</a>, founded in 1918, was among the first modern universities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to teach in an Indian language, Urdu, and its translation bureau rendered large numbers of scientific and legal texts into it. The state also supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dairatul-maarifil-osmania">Dairatul Maarifil Osmania</a>, which published Arabic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, and nobles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salar-jung-iii">Salar Jung III</a> assembled the collections now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salar-jung-museum">Salar Jung Museum</a>. Hyderabad’s alliance with the British in 1798 helped them defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> and exclude the French from the south, and its loyalty in 1857 was an important support of British rule. At the same time, the landlord system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a> and the exclusion of the majority from power fed the communist-led Telangana rebellion and shaped the politics of the region after independence. The fate of the state in 1948 remains a sensitive subject in the history of Indian integration and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>–Muslim relations.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mir-osman-ali-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mir-osman-ali-khan.jpg" alt="Mir Osman Ali Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mir-osman-ali-khan">Mir Osman Ali Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Usman_Ali_Khan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bourne &amp;amp; Shepherd, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The end of British rule in 1947 left Hyderabad, surrounded by the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with a choice between accession and independence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-osman-ali-khan">Mir Osman Ali Khan</a> declared that he would remain independent, and in November 1947 he signed a one-year Standstill Agreement with India while negotiations continued. Inside the state tension mounted. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-majlis-e-ittehadul-muslimeen">Majlis-e-Ittehadul Muslimeen</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qasim-razvi">Qasim Razvi</a> organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/razakars-hyderabad">Razakars</a>, a paramilitary force that attacked opponents of the regime and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> villages, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-state-congress">State Congress</a> campaigned for accession and the Communist Party led a peasant insurrection in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a> against the landlords and the state. On 13 September 1948 the Indian army launched <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-polo">Operation Polo</a>, officially termed a police action, and the Hyderabad forces surrendered on 17 September. The Nizam submitted, and the state was placed under a military governor, Major General J.N. Chaudhuri. The operation and its aftermath were accompanied by large-scale killings of Muslims, which a government-appointed inquiry, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunderlal-committee">Sunderlal Committee</a>, estimated in the tens of thousands. From 1950 Hyderabad was a state of the Indian Union with Osman Ali Khan as its <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajpramukh">Rajpramukh</a></em>, until the reorganisation of 1956 divided it on linguistic lines among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a> and Mysore State. Its Telugu districts today form the core of the state of Telangana.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Hyderabad, the largest and wealthiest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, carried Mughal government and Persianate court culture into the twentieth century under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-jahi-dynasty">Asaf Jahi</a> nizams, and its forcible integration into India in 1948 remains one of the most debated episodes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a>.</em></p>

